=== Filtering ===
NVD family: 30 items, all HIGH/CRITICAL CVSS (min_cvss 8.5 floor already applied by collector).
Checked each for a vendor-of-interest token per the NVD-only-drop rule:
- 29/30 had no genuine vendor-of-interest match. One item (CVE-2026-14890, SGLang ZeroMQ
  auth-bypass) was auto-tagged vendor="Socket" by the extractor, but the article body is about
  SGLang's own backup subsystem, not Socket's product — false-positive substring match, not a
  real Socket-relevant story. Treated as an NVD-only drop per the "generic library CVE unless
  tied to a named enterprise breach" rule.
- 10 of the 30 are a single OpenClaw release's CVE wave (auth bypass / privilege escalation
  across device-pairing, exec approvals, browser routes). No vendor-of-interest tie-in, no named
  enterprise breach attached — dropped as a block.
- Net: 30/30 NVD items dropped at the filter stage. None came close enough to a vendor-of-interest
  token to flag as a near-miss beyond the Socket false positive above.
- Observation for the user: several vendor tags across both NVD and news items (e.g. "SEC", "SSE"
  appearing as vendors on unrelated stories) look like substring false positives out of the vendor
  extractor in src/normalize.py — not touched this run per scope discipline, but worth a look.

=== Clustering (news/hn/youtube, 54 items after NVD drop) ===
Rules applied in order (CVE match -> URL match -> fuzzy title >=85 within 48h -> shared vendor
token + 24h window). Notable clusters:

1. Fairlife/Coca-Cola ransomware — merged 4 items via fuzzy title + same-incident content read:
   bleepingcomputer, securityweek, techcrunch_security, the_record. All published within a
   ~17h window, all describing the same Fairlife production halt. Highest source diversity
   of any cluster this run within the "news" family.

2. SharePoint CVE-2026-58644 KEV zero-day — merged 2 items (thehackernews, securityweek) via
   vendor token match (Microsoft/SharePoint) + same-day timestamps; thehackernews carries the
   CVE ID explicitly, securityweek's "Fresh SharePoint Vulnerability Exploited Soon After
   Disclosure" piece was confirmed as the same underlying flaw by content, not just title fuzz
   (title similarity alone was below the 85 threshold — merged on vendor+timeframe rule 4
   plus manual content read).

3. Fortinet FortiSandbox KEV — single bleepingcomputer item, no cross-posting found elsewhere
   in this run's collection window.

4. Ernst & Young breach — single bleepingcomputer item.

5. CMMC Phase 2 suspension (securityweek) — single item. Note: commercial_phrases in
   config/ranking.yaml does not include "CMMC" itself (has FedRAMP, StateRAMP, IL5, IL6, DORA,
   NIS2, SEC 8-K, etc.) so this cluster scored 0 on commercial_signal despite being squarely a
   regulatory/compliance-deadline story per editorial angle #3. Flagging as a config gap —
   consider adding "CMMC" to commercial_phrases. Not changed this run (scope discipline).

6. Beacon Security $13M raise, Risk Ledger $32M Series B raise (securityweek) — legitimate
   commercial_signal phrase hits ("Raises $", "Series B") but neither company is a vendor of
   interest and neither is a household name for this audience — scored but not competitive
   with the top 5, held as bench candidates.

7. New Windows LegacyHive zero-day (bleepingcomputer) — real zero-day, Microsoft-adjacent, but
   no CVE assigned yet and no KEV/CISA action to hang urgency on — held as a bench candidate,
   lower recency/severity than the two CISA KEV picks above.

8. 1M+ Emails Use Hidden Text to Dupe AI Security Filters (darkreading) — good angle for
   Proofpoint/Mimecast/Abnormal resellers but single-source and no named vendor tie-in in the
